statement:
Let $C\subset\mathbb R\times\mathbb R^d$ be a full-dimensional $(d+1)$-simplex of ambient volume $V$, $d\ge1$, $m=d+1$, with vertex heights $h_i$. At an interior non-knot height $s$ let $I=\{i:h_i<s\}$, $J=\{j:h_j>s\}$, $\delta_i=|h_i-s|$. For $\alpha\in\Delta_{|I|-1}$ and $\beta\in\Delta_{|J|-1}$ write $L=\sum_{i\in I}\alpha_i/\delta_i+\sum_{j\in J}\beta_j/\delta_j$. The section is parametrized in barycentrics by $\lambda_i=\alpha_i/(\delta_i L)$ for $i\in I$ and $\lambda_j=\beta_j/(\delta_j L)$ for $j\in J$. For an ambient affine functional with vertex values $a_i$, the $d$-volume of its closed upper cap at threshold $b$ on this section is
$$
\frac{m!V}{\prod_i\delta_i}
\int_{\Delta_{|I|-1}\times\Delta_{|J|-1}}
\mathbf 1\!\left\{\sum_{i\in I}\frac{(a_i-b)\alpha_i}{\delta_i}
+\sum_{j\in J}\frac{(a_j-b)\beta_j}{\delta_j}\ge0\right\} L^{-m}\,d\alpha\,d\beta .
$$
The simplex measures here are coordinate Lebesgue measures of total masses $1/(|I|-1)!$ and $1/(|J|-1)!$, with mass one in dimension zero. Dropping the indicator gives the whole slice volume $w(s)$. For nonvertical caps use continuous limits at interior knots and inward limits at endpoints. For vertical caps use $w(s)$ times the exact closed base indicator, including at knots and endpoints. The sum over the original integer heights is the raw mixed mass.
